Here is the detailed architectural and execution breakdown for Day 3: Customer Profile + Address, scoped for a Next.js App Router and Supabase stack.

1. Profile Page UI & Data Fetching
1.1 SSR Data Fetching: Create the /customer/profile/page.tsx route. Use a Server Component to fetch the authenticated user's current data from the users (or customers) table during the initial render.

1.2 Profile Form Component: Build a client-side ProfileForm component using Shadcn UI, React Hook Form, and Zod.

Fields: Full Name, Mobile Number, Dietary Preferences/Allergies.

Read-only Fields: Email (since updating email requires a separate secure auth flow).

1.3 Update Server Action: Create an updateProfileAction to securely update the profile records in the database and call revalidatePath('/customer/profile') to refresh the UI.

1.4 UI Polish: Add loading states (spinners or skeleton loaders) and toast notifications for success/error handling upon submission.

2. Address CRUD (Database & Interface)
2.1 Schema & RLS: Create the addresses table in Supabase.

Columns: id, user_id (foreign key), tag (Home/Work/Other), street_1, street_2, landmark, city, state, pincode, is_primary (boolean), is_backup (boolean).

Security: Write Row Level Security (RLS) policies ensuring customers can only SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE their own addresses.

2.2 Read (The Address Grid): Build an AddressList component that displays saved addresses as a grid of Shadcn Cards.

2.3 Create & Update (The Modal Form): Build an AddressFormModal (using Shadcn Dialog/Sheet). Re-use the same form for both adding a new address and editing an existing one by passing an initialData prop.

2.4 Delete Logic: Add a delete button to the address cards with a confirmation dialog (AlertDialog) and a deleteAddressAction Server Action.

3. Location Validation (Serviceable Areas)
3.1 Pincode Allowlist: Create a config/serviceable-areas.ts file containing an array of valid Hyderabad/Telangana pincodes where the diet deliveries are currently active.

3.2 Frontend Zod Validation: Add a .refine() rule to your Zod address schema. If the user enters a pincode not in the allowlist, immediately show a form error: "Sorry, we do not deliver to this area yet."

3.3 Backend Security Guard: Duplicate this pincode check inside your insertAddress Server Action to ensure malicious users cannot bypass the client-side form validation.

4. Primary & Backup Routing Logic
4.1 UI Toggles: Add intuitive UI controls (like a "Set as Default" button or Shadcn Switch toggles) on the address cards to mark them as Primary or Backup.

4.2 The "Single Truth" Server Action: Create a setAddressStatusAction. This needs careful database logic. If a user sets "Address B" as Primary, the action must first execute an UPDATE to set is_primary = false for all other addresses belonging to that user, before setting is_primary = true for Address B.

4.3 Edge Case Handling: Write logic to prevent a user from setting the same address as both Primary and Backup simultaneously.